<h2 id="10h-qigong-in-ytt" class="reveal">Qigong, Energy Cultivation and Relevant Aspects of TCM</h2>
<p style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.1em;color:#5A9EAD;" class="reveal">A 10-hour module within the UNI YOGA 500-Hour Yoga Teacher Training Course</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:1.2em;" class="reveal"><a href="/why-qigong-training/" style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:0.82em;letter-spacing:1.5px;text-transform:uppercase;">→ Why choose this training?</a></p>

<div class="shared-story reveal">
<p>This focused 10-hour module introduces trainee yoga teachers to the principles of Qigong and the foundations of Traditional Chinese Medicine that directly relate to energy cultivation. Offered as part of the UNI YOGA 500-Hour Yoga Teacher Training Course, it bridges two ancient Asian traditions that share a common concern: the cultivation, circulation and preservation of vital energy.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="reveal">Module Content</h3>

<h4 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.1em;color:#2E5A6B;margin-top:1.2em;margin-bottom:0.4em;" class="reveal">Practice</h4>
<ul class="features reveal">
<li>Thorough practice of an Active Qigong Five Element Control Cycle form, utilising Wuji and working across all meridians</li>
<li>Internal work: using the Yi (mind) to guide the energy</li>
</ul>

<h4 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.1em;color:#2E5A6B;margin-top:1.2em;margin-bottom:0.4em;" class="reveal">Foundations of Qigong and TCM</h4>
<ul class="features reveal">
<li>Introduction to Qigong as a branch of Traditional Chinese Medicine</li>
<li>Qi and its relationship to the yogic concept of Prana</li>
<li>The Three Treasures (San Bao) and their correspondence with the Pancha Kosha model</li>
<li>The Three Dantian and their correspondence with key Chakra locations</li>
<li>The philosophical roots shared by Qigong and Yoga as contemplative energy practices</li>
</ul>

<h4 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.1em;color:#2E5A6B;margin-top:1.2em;margin-bottom:0.4em;" class="reveal">The Meridian System</h4>
<ul class="features reveal">
<li>Structure and function of the Jing Luo (meridian and channel) system</li>
<li>The 12 primary meridians and their associated organ systems</li>
<li>The 8 extraordinary vessels, with particular focus on the Ren Mai and Du Mai</li>
<li>Comparison of the meridian system with the Nadi system in yogic anatomy</li>
</ul>

<h4 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.1em;color:#2E5A6B;margin-top:1.2em;margin-bottom:0.4em;" class="reveal">Acupoints</h4>
<ul class="features reveal">
<li>What acupoints (Xue Wei) are and how they function within the meridian system</li>
<li>Key acupoints relevant to Qigong self-cultivation and their locations</li>
<li>Categories of acupoints and their therapeutic significance</li>
<li>Comparison with the Marma points of Ayurveda and the Chakra system</li>
</ul>

<h4 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.1em;color:#2E5A6B;margin-top:1.2em;margin-bottom:0.4em;" class="reveal">Five Element Theory</h4>
<ul class="features reveal">
<li>The Wu Xing (Five Element) theory and its correspondences</li>
<li>The Sheng (generating) and Ke (controlling) cycles</li>
<li>Applying Five Element theory to Qigong practices and forms</li>
<li>Comparison of Wu Xing with the Pancha Mahabhuta (five great elements) of Yoga and Ayurveda</li>
</ul>

<h4 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.1em;color:#2E5A6B;margin-top:1.2em;margin-bottom:0.4em;" class="reveal">Integration with Yoga</h4>
<ul class="features reveal">
<li>How classical Qigong forms activate the meridian system and stimulate acupoints</li>
<li>Connections between Qigong meditation methods and the higher limbs of Raja Yoga (Pratyahara, Dharana, Dhyana)</li>
<li>An integrated view of Chinese and Indian energy systems for teaching</li>
<li>Ethical considerations when teaching across traditions</li>
</ul>

<h3 class="reveal">Who Is This For</h3>
<ul class="features reveal">
<li>Trainee teachers enrolled in the UNI YOGA 500-Hour Yoga Teacher Training Course</li>
<li>Yoga practitioners who want to understand the energetic dimensions of their practice more deeply</li>
<li>Teachers looking to bridge yogic and Chinese traditions in their work</li>
</ul>

<p class="reveal">This module is delivered as part of the UNI YOGA 500-Hour Yoga Teacher Training Course. For enrolment in the full training, please <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a>.</p>

